\section{Single-Signal Selection Results}\label{sec:selection_gap}

\paragraph{Definitional vs.\ empirical.}
Four of five signals are instance-level aggregates that assign identical scores to all $N$ candidates from a given input (\S\ref{sec:formulation}); selection failure is a design property.
LP, the only per-sample signal, suffers compression under constrained decoding, with 60\% of SciERC instances showing within-instance LP range below 0.041 nats (Appendix~\ref{app:logprob_ties}). This compression is model-intrinsic (free-form std ratio $0.97{\times}$; Appendices~\ref{app:freeform_ablation},~\ref{app:lp_failure_stability}); where LP varies, gains depend on alignment, not degeneracy.
Within-instance $\rho$ measures whether LP discriminates candidates for a fixed input (median $0.26$ on SciERC vs.\ $0.67$ on Few-NERD; Appendix~\ref{app:within_instance_rho}), making it the relevant predictor of selection success.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ selection (macro F1) on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Selection signals: 2-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$; baselines: 3-seed (Oracle${=}0.775$, Greedy${=}0.645$). LP is per-sample; others use consensus-proximity. $^\dagger$One score per input.}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & $p$ \\
\midrule
Oracle & $0.775 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
Greedy ($T{=}0$) & $0.645 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
\midrule
LP           & $\mathbf{0.647} \pm 0.005$ & .29 \\
SJ           & $0.646 \pm 0.002$ & .67 \\
FK           & $0.646 \pm 0.001$ & .79 \\
EM           & $0.637 \pm 0.001$ & n/a$^\dagger$ \\
VC           & $0.623 \pm 0.004$ & n/a$^\dagger$ \\
\midrule
Random       & $0.610 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\columnwidth]{fig_selection_gap}
\caption{Correlation-selection gap on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Bars: QE $\rho$; line: $\Delta$F1 vs.\ greedy (3-seed mean). Despite $\rho$ up to 0.38, no signal outperforms greedy (all $p{\geq}0.29$). Dashed green: oracle headroom ($+13.0$\,pp; oracle headroom $=$ best-of-$N$ F1 $-$ greedy F1); dotted gray: random.}
\label{fig:selection_gap}
\end{figure}

Despite $\rho$ up to 0.38, no signal outperforms greedy on SciERC (Table~\ref{tab:selection}; Figure~\ref{fig:selection_gap}); the oracle shows 13.0\,pp headroom, but LP achieves $+0.2$\,pp ($p{=}0.29$), below the MDE (${\approx}$1.5\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}). TOST confirms equivalence within ${\pm}2$--$5$\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:tost}); multiple comparison corrections in Appendix~\ref{app:multiple_comparisons}.
The pattern holds across CoNLL and WNUT-17 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}); insufficient diversity is not the explanation, as 42\% of SciERC instances contain oracle-improvable samples with $+$31.9\,pp mean headroom (Appendix~\ref{app:diversity}).
Few-NERD provides the clearest positive case: LP achieves $+1.39$\,pp ($p{<}0.001$; Holm $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.021$, selection family $k{=}21$)\footnote{Under global correction ($k{=}57$), LP-best selection reaches Holm $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.055$ (Benjamini--Hochberg [BH] $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.003$).} at low degeneracy (13.0\% vs.\ 12.2--67.9\%), while structural signals still degrade (VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$\,pp; \S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).

\paragraph{Diagnostic framework.}
Three co-occurring factors jointly characterize the gap:
(1)~instance degeneracy sets the ceiling (SciERC 12.2\%, CoNLL 61.7\%);
(2)~oracle headroom bounds the opportunity within non-degenerate instances (CoNLL ${\approx}4$\,pp vs.\ SciERC 13.0\,pp);
(3)~LP compression limits exploitation (Few-NERD within-instance $\rho{\approx}0.5$--$0.7$ vs.\ SciERC $\rho{\approx}0.26$).
Degeneracy does not predict LP gain across datasets ($\rho{=}0.052$, $n{=}23$, $p{=}0.81$; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), though it co-varies with LP within fixed settings ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).
These conditions interact ($R^2{<}0$); on non-degenerate instances, $\alpha$ approaches zero ($0.000$--$0.121$) despite $\rho$ up to 0.45 (\S\ref{sec:theory}). VC quartile stratification confirms complementary failures (Q4: low headroom; Q1--Q2: unreliable LP; Appendix~\ref{app:vc_quartile}); entity-token LP achieves higher $\rho$ (0.44 vs.\ 0.30) yet worse selection (Appendix~\ref{app:entity_token_lp}).

\paragraph{Mechanistic evidence (Appendix~\ref{sec:mechanistic}).}
Why does signal alignment collapse? Probing experiments on Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct reveal an \textit{introspection boundary}: hidden-state linear probes achieve entity-level AUROC $0.63$--$0.88$, substantially exceeding prompt-based self-judgment (ESJ AUROC $0.51$; confirmed on Qwen3-8B: AUROC $0.50$--$0.55$; Appendix~\ref{app:esj}), yet cross-dataset transfer drops to $0.56$--$0.67$ (leave-one-dataset-out [LODO]).
Entity self-judgment, multi-round refinement ($-3.1$\,pp vs.\ greedy), and latent centroid selection ($-3.5$\,pp) all fail to improve over greedy decoding.
Entity quality is partially encoded in representations but inaccessible through the generation interface; cross-model validation confirms this is architecture-general (Qwen3-8B ESJ AUROC $0.50$--$0.55$; Appendix~\ref{app:esj}).

